  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 123 AM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nFor the middle to end of this week and into next weekend, surface \nand low level high pressure centered over the western Atlantic will \nbegin to expand back over the region and cause winds to shift back \nto an easterly or east-southeast direction. This easterly to east-\nsoutheast regime will produce a pattern that is common in the \nsummertime across South Florida with convection being driven by the \nsea breeze circulations and being steered towards the Gulf coast \nareas. At the same time, the weak SAL will dissipate by mid-week and \nallow for the deeper moisture to be utilized for widespread shower \nand thunderstorm development. At the same time, we will be \nmonitoring for some weak disturbances and vorticity maxima forming \noff the Caribbean islands as much of the guidance is showing \npotential for these weak lows to form, mainly in the mid-levels\nand advect close to the region. Nevertheless, abundant moisture \n(PWATs 1.8-2.0+\") will be present for Wed-Fri and into the weekend\nfor any vorticity and diurnal heating instability to take\nadvantage of. Highest rain chances (60-80%) each day will be \nacross interior and Southwest Florida as the easterly to east- \nsoutheast wind regime favors these areas receiving the most \nwidespread rain.\n\nHigh temperatures are projected to remain in the low to mid 90s each \nday. Overnight lows range from the low 70s across the interior and \nlake regions to the upper 70s and low 80s along the coastal\nmetros.",
